1
00:00:08,582 --> 00:00:15,732
All right, let's jump into our first
overview of a routing protocol.

2
00:00:15,732 --> 00:00:20,160
We're going to focus in on
link state protocols today, or

3
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,000
one specific link state protocol.

4
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:29,876
So as a reminder, we talked about
a decentralized control plane.

5
00:00:29,876 --> 00:00:35,164
And so this is what you would traditional
generally know as a routing protocol.

6
00:00:35,164 --> 00:00:40,779
And this is where each router runs
some control plane software and

7
00:00:40,779 --> 00:00:45,985
then communicates with its neighbors and
then performs some

8
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:52,266
local calculations to figure out
what the forwarding table should be.

9
00:00:52,266 --> 00:00:57,112
And we went over kind of three
key properties that distinguish

10
00:00:57,112 --> 00:00:59,748
different routing protocols.

11
00:00:59,748 --> 00:01:04,501
And so keep these in mind as
we go through this today,

12
00:01:04,501 --> 00:01:11,091
what info each router knows,
what information each router exchanges,

13
00:01:11,091 --> 00:01:15,496
and then what calculation
is being performed.

14
00:01:15,496 --> 00:01:21,360
So think about those questions as we go
through kind of the rest of this video.

15
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:26,332
So what we're going to talk
about is a protocol called OSPF.

16
00:01:26,332 --> 00:01:29,655
This is an example of
a link state protocol and

17
00:01:29,655 --> 00:01:32,956
it's widely used in local area networks.

18
00:01:32,956 --> 00:01:41,584
So you can find the full specification
of OSPF in these two links.

19
00:01:41,584 --> 00:01:45,872
So these are something called RFCs.

20
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:50,117
And so
this is where a lot of the information or

21
00:01:50,117 --> 00:01:57,163
the kind of specifications for internet
related technology gets placed and

22
00:01:57,163 --> 00:02:03,200
then it has an ability for
a community to suggest comments.

23
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,904
So you can see it's beefy.

24
00:02:06,904 --> 00:02:12,598
244 pages is not an easy read.

25
00:02:12,598 --> 00:02:17,862
That said, we're going to overview,
give a little highlight of it.

26
00:02:17,862 --> 00:02:22,873
We're not going to give you enough
detail where cover all of that and

27
00:02:22,873 --> 00:02:27,794
all the different kind of corner cases and
things that it covers,

28
00:02:27,794 --> 00:02:32,621
but you'll at least have a good
sense of how OSPF is designed and

29
00:02:32,621 --> 00:02:37,480
then be able to answer those
three questions at the beginning.

30
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:43,331
So the process in OSPF,
if we were to simplify it,

31
00:02:43,331 --> 00:02:48,496
each router establishes
peering with its neighbors,

32
00:02:48,496 --> 00:02:54,514
then link states and
we'll discuss what that exactly means.

33
00:02:54,514 --> 00:02:59,609
So link states are flooded
to the entire network and

34
00:02:59,609 --> 00:03:04,480
then each router calculates
the shortest path.

35
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:08,742
All right, so what is a link state?

36
00:03:08,742 --> 00:03:13,960
So a link state is basically
the state of a link.

37
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:16,624
So each of the interfaces for

38
00:03:16,624 --> 00:03:21,978
any given network device has
some information about it.

39
00:03:21,978 --> 00:03:27,445
So if it's connected to another
router well, the link state,

40
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:32,574
the information about that
link is who that neighbor is.

41
00:03:32,574 --> 00:03:37,603
And if it's connected to
a network with end hosts

42
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:43,752
that link state tells about
what prefixes are reachable.

43
00:03:43,752 --> 00:03:48,314
So you can look at kind of
router A as an example,

44
00:03:48,314 --> 00:03:54,131
it has connection on the left
to these different prefixes and

45
00:03:54,131 --> 00:04:00,080
then it has connection to the right
to connection to a router.

46
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:07,354
So showing this, the link states for
two different routers, A and C.

47
00:04:07,354 --> 00:04:12,805
So if we put some interface
numbers behind these routers,

48
00:04:12,805 --> 00:04:17,477
so interface A we already
kind of talked about, but

49
00:04:17,477 --> 00:04:24,542
interface0 is connected to Prefix-1,
interface1 is connected to B.

50
00:04:24,542 --> 00:04:27,852
So that would be the link state for

51
00:04:27,852 --> 00:04:32,829
router A,
the different link states that it has.

52
00:04:32,829 --> 00:04:37,470
C would be 1 that interface0,
says C is connected to B and

53
00:04:37,470 --> 00:04:41,661
interface1, the link state
is C is connected to D.

54
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:48,758
So then the next part of OSPF
is exchanging information

55
00:04:48,758 --> 00:04:50,748
with your neighbors.

56
00:04:50,748 --> 00:04:54,800
And so what they do is they
exchange link state updates.

57
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:59,646
And this is basically just tell
the neighbors all of the link states that

58
00:04:59,646 --> 00:05:01,480
you know about.

59
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:09,580
So starting off, A will tell B
that A is connected to Prefix-1.

60
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:14,251
Because that was one of the link
states that A was storing.

61
00:05:14,251 --> 00:05:19,437
C will tell B that C is connected to D and

62
00:05:19,437 --> 00:05:27,600
then D will tell B that D is
connected to C and Prefix-2.

63
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:33,449
So this was kind of one step of the link
state update process of in the kind

64
00:05:33,449 --> 00:05:39,704
of viewpoint of if I'm router B,
here's what my neighbors are telling me.

65
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:46,804
B then now has more information
about all of the networks,

66
00:05:46,804 --> 00:05:50,178
all these link states that it receives,

67
00:05:50,178 --> 00:05:54,720
it stores this all in
a little database internally.

68
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:58,620
And so this database consists
of the link states for

69
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:03,970
its interfaces as well as all of
the link states that it has learned from

70
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:09,614
the messages from A, C and D,
whatever that information that it sent.

71
00:06:09,614 --> 00:06:15,192
And now B can now send a link
state update to its neighbors.

72
00:06:15,192 --> 00:06:20,554
So B would tell A for example,
I know about the link state

73
00:06:20,554 --> 00:06:25,606
that C is connected to D and
that C is connected to B.

74
00:06:25,606 --> 00:06:29,185
Those are some information
that it can send and

75
00:06:29,185 --> 00:06:33,314
then it would also the information
it learned from D,

76
00:06:33,314 --> 00:06:40,520
the link state of D is that D is connected
to C and that D is connected to Prefix-2.

77
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:45,952
So all of this happens,
all of these routers just keep exchanging

78
00:06:45,952 --> 00:06:53,236
this link state information until the
network reaches what's called convergence.

79
00:06:53,236 --> 00:06:58,191
And that's where basically
everybody has agreed upon this is

80
00:06:58,191 --> 00:07:01,680
all of the information in the network.

81
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:07,288
They all have basically the same database,
the link state database at each router.

82
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,294
This is what we just said.

83
00:07:14,294 --> 00:07:18,665
B will send this link state update to
its neighbors with new information,

84
00:07:18,665 --> 00:07:21,804
process continues until
it reaches convergence.

85
00:07:21,804 --> 00:07:26,712
So the process just keeps going and
going until you reach convergence.

86
00:07:26,712 --> 00:07:30,704
Now at the converged state of the network.

87
00:07:30,704 --> 00:07:34,640
So after that process is completed,
at the end,

88
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:40,356
if we were to again focus in on B,
it has learned the entire topology.

89
00:07:40,356 --> 00:07:43,719
And so you can kind of see in
this little representation,

90
00:07:43,719 --> 00:07:49,020
it knows the entire topology because it
was told all of these various link states.

91
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:52,182
And if we were to zoom in
on any of the routers,

92
00:07:52,182 --> 00:07:55,378
we would see that exact same information.

93
00:07:55,378 --> 00:07:58,487
They all agree on that this
is the state of the network.

94
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:07,580
All right, so now that we have that,
how does this help us forward traffic?

95
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:09,431
Really what we want is for

96
00:08:09,431 --> 00:08:15,350
the source on the left to be able to send
traffic to the destination on the right.

97
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,722
So this is where the calculation that.

98
00:08:19,722 --> 00:08:24,040
Each router is performing, how are they
determining the forwarding table?

99
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:29,722
In OSPF, each router performs
a shortest path calculation and

100
00:08:29,722 --> 00:08:33,900
this is just Dijkstra's algorithm.

101
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:38,486
So recall we mentioned before
that a network is just

102
00:08:38,486 --> 00:08:41,320
a representation of a graph.

103
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:46,146
And so shown here, each of these kind of

104
00:08:46,146 --> 00:08:51,265
blue dots can look at them as routers, and

105
00:08:51,265 --> 00:08:58,620
then the edges between the nodes or
vertices are weights.

106
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:05,605
And weights could come into play in
a network graph of the link bandwidth or

107
00:09:05,605 --> 00:09:13,080
latency on the link or any number of
factors that you want to factor in.

108
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:20,140
All right, so let's look at
Dijkstra's algorithm in action.

109
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:26,694
So what we're trying to
calculate here is the shortest

110
00:09:26,694 --> 00:09:32,962
path from node U to each of
the other destinations w,

111
00:09:32,962 --> 00:09:38,373
v, x, y, s, t, z because that's what will

112
00:09:38,373 --> 00:09:43,230
help us build the forwarding table in u.

113
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:51,460
So it starts by saying, all right what are
the immediate next potential next hops?

114
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:55,450
And it picks the kind of lowest cost one.

115
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:58,760
So now the lowest cost one would be w.

116
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:03,194
So now the Dijkstra's algorithm, and

117
00:10:03,194 --> 00:10:08,700
you can look up on the internet for
a more specific

118
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:15,572
kind of look at what Dijkstra's
algorithm is more concrete.

119
00:10:15,572 --> 00:10:19,364
This is Dijkstra's algorithm
through example, but

120
00:10:19,364 --> 00:10:24,504
you can look up what the actual algorithm
is widely available to look at,

121
00:10:24,504 --> 00:10:29,602
and there's a lot of resources out
there explaining it pretty well.

122
00:10:29,602 --> 00:10:33,471
So in step 1,
we started at u the next hop or

123
00:10:33,471 --> 00:10:38,630
the next node to look at was
whatever was the lowest cost so

124
00:10:38,630 --> 00:10:44,406
then that's where w, we chose w,
and now we're looking at w.

125
00:10:44,406 --> 00:10:48,422
So we add to the possible nodes
that we could select from.

126
00:10:48,422 --> 00:10:53,050
So now we have v, x and s because we just

127
00:10:53,050 --> 00:10:57,681
added w's neighbors, which are x and

128
00:10:57,681 --> 00:11:02,860
s, to the set that we can select from.

129
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:10,610
And then we go ahead and choose what
is the kind of lowest cost one.

130
00:11:10,610 --> 00:11:15,415
So you can see the cost to get to v was 3,

131
00:11:15,415 --> 00:11:19,638
the cost to get to x was also 3, and

132
00:11:19,638 --> 00:11:25,078
then the cost to get to s was 6, 2 plus 4.

133
00:11:25,078 --> 00:11:29,092
All right, so
now you have a choice between x or v,

134
00:11:29,092 --> 00:11:32,500
either one, they're equal cost.

135
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:37,403
So in this illustration,
we chose v as the next node to add

136
00:11:37,403 --> 00:11:42,280
to the graph or
to the set of nodes that we can look at.

137
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:48,324
So we looked at v and
then add to the set its neighbors.

138
00:11:48,324 --> 00:11:52,645
So when we look at one
of the neighbors is x,

139
00:11:52,645 --> 00:11:57,808
we see that it's already in the set so
we don't need

140
00:11:57,808 --> 00:12:03,932
to add it because going u, v,
x would be a longer path than u,

141
00:12:03,932 --> 00:12:08,680
w, x,
which we already have kind of covered.

142
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:13,482
So the only node that would get
kind of added to the set that we

143
00:12:13,482 --> 00:12:18,187
are looking at or able to look
at in the next round is y and so

144
00:12:18,187 --> 00:12:22,118
then we'll add that
the cost to get to y is 2.

145
00:12:22,118 --> 00:12:26,474
So now we can choose between y, x and s.

146
00:12:26,474 --> 00:12:31,002
So it cost 6, cost 3, and cost 5.

147
00:12:31,002 --> 00:12:37,240
So you could probably guess which
node we look at next, which is x.

148
00:12:37,240 --> 00:12:41,276
It adds its neighbors z and t updates,

149
00:12:41,276 --> 00:12:45,186
kind of the cost to get each of those and

150
00:12:45,186 --> 00:12:50,160
then kind of keep
building this whole tree.

151
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:55,912
All right, so we would kind of finish
out that whole set of calculation.

152
00:12:55,912 --> 00:13:00,954
We've now built a tree rooted at u that

153
00:13:00,954 --> 00:13:07,740
includes the shortest path
to every single node.

154
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:14,252
So any node that you want to look at,
u, w, x, t would be the shortest path.

155
00:13:14,252 --> 00:13:18,231
So now, based on that calculation at u,

156
00:13:18,231 --> 00:13:24,435
the forwarding table,
now it knows exactly which direction,

157
00:13:24,435 --> 00:13:30,718
which port it should, for
any given destination, send out.

158
00:13:30,718 --> 00:13:34,222
So if it was looking at,
say destination z,

159
00:13:34,222 --> 00:13:38,765
we would be able to look up in
the table and say it's u, v,

160
00:13:38,765 --> 00:13:43,848
which matches the kind of
calculation that we just performed.

161
00:13:43,848 --> 00:13:47,160
So we perform this calculation
to fill this table.

162
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:51,531
And now when packets come into you say,
destined for Z,

163
00:13:51,531 --> 00:13:57,297
which would have an IP address, we would
be able to look up that address z,

164
00:13:57,297 --> 00:14:00,000
and then say, that's u, v.

165
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:07,488
So that's the essence of OSPF and
Dijkstra's algorithm.

166
00:14:07,488 --> 00:14:11,376
Now, of course,
now there can be dynamics in the network.

167
00:14:11,376 --> 00:14:13,856
There can be changes that occur.

168
00:14:13,856 --> 00:14:16,048
So we might plug in a new router.

169
00:14:16,048 --> 00:14:21,006
So there may be new neighbors that
occur that we need to account for.

170
00:14:21,006 --> 00:14:27,100
There may be a failure of a link or
a node, we might add a prefix.

171
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:30,720
So all of these are changes
in the link state.

172
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:35,462
So from the perspective of this top
router, let's say this link went down

173
00:14:35,462 --> 00:14:39,192
well now its link state that
it knows about just changed.

174
00:14:39,192 --> 00:14:46,100
It knows now top router is no longer
connected to bottom right router.

175
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:51,160
And so it would send an update
to this bottom left router

176
00:14:51,160 --> 00:14:58,016
to say here's the link states that I
know of and so it does these updates.

177
00:14:58,016 --> 00:15:02,544
And again, we go through that
process until convergence.

178
00:15:02,544 --> 00:15:07,519
We calculate the shortest paths and
then update the forwarding table.

179
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:14,313
So that's it with OSPF,
it's pretty straightforward if you

180
00:15:14,313 --> 00:15:17,669
understand how Dijkstra's
algorithm works and

181
00:15:17,669 --> 00:15:21,820
then understand what exactly
we mean by link state.

182
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:26,866
All right, so next time we're going to
talk about a path vector protocol

183
00:15:26,866 --> 00:15:32,610
called BGP and it's really what runs
the internet, so should be interesting.